hing through marked.\n\nActually wait, I just realized something about the empty content test:\n```typescript\nit('handles empty content', async () => {\n    const file = makeFile({ content: '' });\n    const result = await plugin.hooks.transformContent!(file, makeCtx());\n    expect(result.content).toBeDefined();\n});\n```\nSo it just needs to not throw. marked.parse('') should return '' as a string, so that should work.\n\nOK let me also think about the `hasMarkdown` variable I added - actually, I don't need it since marked will handle HTML content fine. Let me remove it.\n\nNow let me re-examine the footnote definition regex. Looking at the test input:\n```\nSome text[^1] here.\n\n[^1]: This is the footnote.\n```\n\nIf I run `/\\[\\^(\\d+)\\]:\\s*([^\\n]*)/g` on this, it matches `[^1]: This is the footnote.` and replaces it with '', leaving `Some text[^1] here.\\n\\n`. Then the `[^1]` reference is still there and gets picked up by the reference regex. Good.\n\nBut wait, what about the second test:\n```\nText[^1] and more[^2].\n\n[^1]: First note.\n[^2]: Second note.\n```\n\nThe regex would match `[^1]: First note.` first (replacing with ''), then `[^2]: Second note.` (replacing with ''). So `Text[^1] and more[^2].\\n\\n` is left. Then the reference regex picks up `[^1]` and `[^2]`. Good.\n\nBut what about the newline handling? After removing the footnote definitions, we might have extra blank lines. That's fine - marked handles extra blank lines.\n\nHmm, but actually looking at the test output again for footnotes:\n```typescript\nit('renders footnote section at bottom of content', async () => {\n    const file = makeFile({ content: 'Text[^1] and more[^2].\\n\\n[^1]: First note.\\n[^2]: Second note.' });\n    const result = await plugin.hooks.transformContent!(file, makeCtx());\n    expect(result.content).toContain('<section class=\"footnotes\">');\n    expect(result.content).toContain('<li id=\"fn-1\">First note.</li>');\n    expect(result.content).toContain('<li id=\"fn-2\">Second note.</li>');\n});\n```\n\nNote that the `<li id=\"fn-1\">` includes the note text. So the footnote text just gets put inside the li directly, not as a paragraph or anything.\n\nLet me also think about whether the indentation of the section matters. The test just checks `toContain`, so as long as these strings appear in the output, it should pass.\n\nOK, let me now also think about the edge case where `marked.parse()` might convert `<div class=\"custom\">Already HTML</div>` to something different. In marked v17, HTML blocks are preserved by default. However, marked might add wrapping `<p>` tags around inline HTML. Let me not worry too much about this and test it when I run the tests.\n\nNow for the asset-pipeline plugin:\n\n### asset-pipeline.ts\n\nLooking at the tests:\n1. Rewrite relative image URLs `./image.png` → `/assets/image.png`\n2. Rewrite relative link URLs `./doc.pdf` → `/assets/doc.pdf`\n3. Leave absolute URLs (`https://`) unchanged\n4. Generate manifest: `{ assets: string[] }` in `ctx.data` as `asset-manifest`\n5. No assets → no change\n6. Multiple assets tracked\n7. Accumulates across files\n8. Already-rewritten URLs (`/assets/image.png`) not double-processed\n\nI need to:\n- Find `<img src=\"./...\"` and `<a href=\"./...\"` patterns\n- Replace with `/assets/...`\n- Extract asset filenames and add to manifest\n\nFor the URL pattern: I need to match `src=\"./...` and `href=\"./...` where the path references a file (has a file extension). Let me use a regex:\n\n```\n/(src|href)=\"\\.\\/([^\"]+\\.\\w+)\"/g\n```\n\nThis matches `src=\"./image.png\"` and `href=\"./doc.pdf\"` but not `src=\"https://...\"` (no `./` prefix) or `src=\"/assets/...\"` (no `./` prefix).\n\nFor already-rewritten URLs: The regex only matches `./` prefix, so `/assets/image.png` won't match. \n\nFor extracting asset filenames: I need to get just the filename (e.g., `image.png` from `./image.png`).\n\nLet me implement:\n\n```typescript\nexport function createAssetPipelinePlugin(): Plugin {\n  return {\n    name: 'asset-pipeline',\n    version: '1.0.0',\n    dependencies: [],\n    hooks: {\n      transformContent(file: ContentFile, ctx: BuildContext): ContentFile {\n        let content = file.content;\n        const assetRegex = /(src|href)=\"\\.\\/([^\"]+\\.\\w+)\"/g;\n        \n        // Get or create manifest\n        let manifest = ctx.data.get('asset-manifest') as { assets: string[] } | undefined;\n        if (!manifest) {\n          manifest = { assets: [] };\n          ctx.data.set('asset-manifest', manifest);\n        }\n        \n        content = content.replace(assetRegex, (_match, attr, assetPath) => {\n          // Add to manifest\n          const filename = assetPath.split('/').pop()!;\n          if (!manifest!.assets.includes(filename)) {\n            manifest!.assets.push(filename);\n          }\n          return `${attr}=\"/assets/${assetPath}\"`;\n        });\n        \n        return { ...file, content };\n      },\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\nWait, let me check the tests more carefully:\n\n```typescript\nit('rewrites relative image URLs to /assets/ path', async () => {\n    const file = makeFile({ content: '<img src=\"./image.png\" alt=\"test\">' });\n    ...\n    expect(result.content).toContain('src=\"/assets/image.png\"');\n    expect(result.content).not.toContain('src=\"./image.png\"');\n});\n```\n\nSo `./image.png` → `/assets/image.png` (the full relative path).\n\n```typescript\nit('handles multiple assets in one file', async () => {\n    const file = makeFile({\n      content: '<img src=\"./a.png\"><img src=\"./b.jpg\"><a href=\"./c.pdf\">link</a>',\n    });\n    ...\n    expect(manifest.assets).toContain('a.png');\n    expect(manifest.assets).toContain('b.jpg');\n    expect(manifest.assets).toContain('c.pdf');\n});\n```\n\nSo the asset manifest stores just the filename (not the full path).\n\nWait, but `./images/photo.jpg` would have the path `images/photo.jpg`. The manifest needs just `photo.jpg`? Let me look again at the test:\n\n```typescript\nit('generates asset manifest in ctx.data', async () => {\n    const file = makeFile({ content: '<img src=\"./photo.jpg\">' });\n    ...\n    expect(manifest.assets).toContain('photo.jpg');\n});\n```\n\nHmm, the asset path is `./photo.jpg`, so the filename would be `photo.jpg`. But what about `./images/photo.jpg`? The manifest would store `photo.jpg`. But wait, my regex matches `\\.\\./([^\"]+\\.\\w+)` - wait no, I'm using `\\.\\/([^\"]+\\.\\w+)`. Let me re-check.\n\nThe regex `(src|href)=\"\\.\\/([^\"]+\\.\\w+)\"` would match `src=\"./image.png\"` and capture `image.png` in group 2. For `src=\"./images/photo.jpg\"`, it would capture `images/photo.jpg`.\n\nActually wait, `\\w+` at the end only matches word characters. What about `.js` files? `\\w+` would match `js`. OK that's fine.\n\nBut the manifest extraction... let me use `assetPath` directly in the URL rewrite but extract just the filename for the manifest. Actually, looking at the tests again - all the asset paths are simple filenames, not nested paths. So `assetPath.split('/').pop()` would work fine.\n\nBut wait, should the manifest track just filenames or the full relative path? Looking at test:\n```typescript\nexpect(manifest.assets).toContain('photo.jpg');\n```\nIt just stores the filename. But what if there are two files with the same name in different directories? That's an edge case not covered by the tests.\n\nLet me keep it simple and just extract the filename.\n\nActually, hmm, I just realized I should check: what about the `pre[data-language]` attribute. Looking at the test for already-rewritten URLs:\n```typescript\nit('does not double-process already-rewritten URLs', async () => {\n    const file = makeFile({ content: '<img src=\"/assets/image.png\">' });\n    const ctx = makeCtx();\n    const result = await plugin.hooks.transformContent!(file, ctx);\n    expect(result.content).toBe('<img src=\"/assets/image.png\">');\n    const manifest = ctx.data.get('asset-manifest') as { assets: string[] } | undefined;\n    expect(!manifest || manifest.assets.length === 0).toBe(true);\n});\n```\n\nSince my regex only matches `./` prefix URLs, this test should pass - `/assets/image.png` won't match.\n\nOK, I think my implementation is correct. Let me move on.\n\n### syntax-highlight.ts\n\nTests:\n1. `<pre><code class=\"language-javascript\">` → class becomes `\"language-javascript highlighted\"` and `data-language=\"javascript\"` on pre\n2. Multiple code blocks each get their language\n3. No language: left unchanged\n4. Inline `<code>` not modified\n5. Content preserved\n\nI need to:\n- Find `<pre><code class=\"language-xxx\">` blocks\n- Update the code tag class to add `highlighted`\n- Add `data-language=\"xxx\"` to the `<pre>` tag\n- Leave `<pre><code>` (no language) unchanged\n- Leave inline `<code>` unchanged\n\nI need to be careful to only match block-level code (inside `<pre>`), not inline `<code>`.\n\nLet me use a regex approach. I need to match `<pre><code class=\"language-...\">...</code></pre>` patterns. But regex for HTML... it can be tricky. Let me think about what the test cases look like:\n\nTest 1: `<pre><code class=\"language-javascript\">const x = 1;</code></pre>`\nOutput should have: `class=\"language-javascript highlighted\"` and `data-language=\"javascript\"` on pre\nSo: `<pre data-language=\"javascript\"><code class=\"language-javascript highlighted\">const x = 1;</code></pre>`\n\nHmm, but the test checks:\n```typescript\nexpect(result.content).toContain('class=\"language-javascript highlighted\"');\nexpect(result.content).toContain('data-language=\"python\"');\n```\n\nAnd also:\n```typescript\nexpect(result.content).not.toContain('highlighted');\n```\nfor no-language blocks.\n\nAnd:\n```typescript\nexpect(result.content).toBe('<p>Use <code>console.log()</code> for debugging.</p>');\n```\nfor inline code.\n\nSo I need to differentiate between `<pre><code class=\"language-...\">` and `<code>` without `<pre>`.\n\nLet me use a regex approach:\n\n```typescript\ncontent = content.replace(\n  /<pre><code class=\"language-(\\w+)\">([\\s\\S]*?)<\\/code><\\/pre>/g,\n  (_, lang, code) => {\n    return `<pre data-language=\"${lang}\"><code class=\"language-${lang} highlighted\">${code}</code></pre>`;\n  }\n);\n```\n\nThis should:\n- Match only `<pre><code class=\"language-xxx\">` patterns (block-level only)\n- Add `data-language` to `<pre>`\n- Add `highlighted` to the class\n- Not match inline `<code>` (no `<pre>` before it)\n- Not match `<pre><code>` without a language class\n\nBut wait, what about `<pre><code class=\"language-js\"></code></pre>` (empty code block)? My regex uses `[\\s\\S]*?` which would match empty content too. Let me verify: `[\\s\\S]*?` matches zero characters. So `<pre><code class=\"language-js\"></code></pre>` should match. Good.\n\nAnd `<pre><code>plain code</code></pre>` - this wouldn't match because there's no `class=\"language-\"` in the string. Good.\n\nLet me also check: what if there's whitespace or newlines between `<pre>` and `<code>`? The test cases don't show this, so I'll assume no whitespace between tags.\n\nNow for the test:\n```typescript\nit('handles code block with no language specified', async () => {\n    const file = makeFile({\n      content: '<pre><code>plain code</code></pre>',\n    });\n    const result = await plugin.hooks.transformContent!(file, makeCtx());\n    expect(result.content).toBe('<pre><code>plain code</code></pre>');\n    expect(result.content).not.toContain('highlighted');\n});\n```\n\nSince my regex requires `class=\"language-...\"`, `<pre><code>plain code</code></pre>` won't match, so it passes through unchanged. \n\nBut wait, what if we have `<pre><code class=\"some-other-class\">`? Looking at the tests, there's no such case, so I'll only handle `class=\"language-xxx\"`.\n\nOK, I think this implementation is correct. Let me now write the taxonomy plugin.\n\n### taxonomy.ts\n\nTests:\n1. `beforeBuild`: Builds tag index from files' tags, stores as `taxonomy-tags` Map<string, ContentFile[]>\n2. `afterBuild`: Generates tag listing pages in ctx.output at `tags/javascript.html`\n3. Handles files with no tags (empty map)\n4. Multiple tags per file\n5. Tags are case-normalized to lowercase\n6. Empty file list\n\nThe tag pages should contain the post titles. Looking at the test:\n```typescript\nexpect(ctx.output.get('tags/javascript.html')).toContain('Post 1');\n```\n\nSo the tag page just needs to contain the post titles. It doesn't specify a template, so I can just generate a simple HTML list.\n\nLet me implement:\n\n```typescript\nexport function createTaxonomyPlugin(): Plugin {\n  return {\n    name: 'taxonomy',\n    version: '1.0.0',\n    dependencies: [],\n    hooks: {\n      beforeBuild(ctx: BuildContext): void {\n        const tagMap = new Map<string, ContentFile[]>();\n        \n        for (const file of ctx.files) {\n          if (file.tags && file.tags.length > 0) {\n            for (const tag of file.tags) {\n              const normalized = tag.toLowerCase();\n              if (!tagMap.has(normalized)) {\n                tagMap.set(normalized, []);\n              }\n              tagMap.get(normalized)!.push(file);\n            }\n          }\n        }\n        \n        ctx.data.set('taxonomy-tags', tagMap);\n      },\n      \n      afterBuild(ctx: BuildContext): void {\n        const tagMap = ctx.data.get('taxonomy-tags') as Map<string, ContentFile[]>;\n        if (!tagMap) return;\n        \n        for (const [tag, files] of tagMap.entries()) {\n          const links = files\n            .map(f => `<li><a href=\"/${f.slug}.html\">${f.title}</a></li>`)\n            .join('\\n');\n          \n          const page = `<h1>Tag: ${tag}</h1>\\n<ul>\\n${links}\\n</ul>`;\n          ctx.output.set(`tags/${tag}.html`, page);\n        }\n      },\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\nWait, looking at the tests:\n```typescript\nit('generates tag listing pages in ctx.output', async () => {\n    const files = [\n      makeFile({ slug: 'post-1', title: 'Post 1', tags: ['javascript'] }),\n    ];\n    const ctx = makeCtx(files);\n    await plugin.hooks.beforeBuild!(ctx);\n    await plugin.hooks.afterBuild!(ctx);\n    expect(ctx.output.has('tags/javascript.html')).toBe(true);\n    expect(ctx.output.get('tags/javascript.html')).toContain('Post 1');\n});\n```\n\nSo the test runs both `beforeBuild` and `afterBuild`. The tag page just needs to contain the post title - no specific HTML structure required.\n\nOK, let me also think about dependencies - taxonomy has none.\n\n### rss-feed.ts\n\nTests:\n1. Generates valid RSS 2.0 XML with `feed.xml`\n2. Items contain HTML content wrapped in `<![CDATA[...]]>`\n3. Items include `<category>` tags from taxonomy data  \n4. Channel title from config\n5. Items sorted by date, newest first\n6. Handles files without dates\n7. Link uses baseUrl\n\nLooking at the test structure, it uses `afterBuild` hook and reads content from `ctx.files` which should have HTML content (because markdown-ext has already run).\n\nDependencies: `['markdown-ext', 'taxonomy']`\n\nImplementation:\n```typescript\nexport function createRssFeedPlugin(): Plugin {\n  return {\n    name: 'rss-feed',\n    version: '1.0.0',\n    dependencies: ['markdown-ext', 'taxonomy'],\n    hooks: {\n      afterBuild(ctx: BuildContext): void {\n        const { config } = ctx;\n        \n        // Sort files by date, newest first (files without dates go last)\n        const sortedFiles = [...ctx.files].sort((a, b) => {\n          if (!a.date && !b.date) return 0;\n          if (!a.date) return 1;\n          if (!b.date) return -1;\n          return new Date(b.date).getTime() - new Date(a.date).getTime();\n        });\n        \n        // Get tag map from taxonomy data\n        const tagMap = ctx.data.get('taxonomy-tags') as Map<string, ContentFile[]> | undefined;\n        \n        const items = sortedFiles.map(file => {\n          // Get tags for this file\n          const tags: string[] = [];\n          if (tagMap) {\n            for (const [tag, taggedFiles] of tagMap.entries()) {\n              if (taggedFiles.some(f => f.slug === file.slug)) {\n                tags.push(tag);\n              }\n            }\n          }\n          \n          const categories = tags.map(tag => `      <category>${escapeXml(tag)}</category>`).join('\\n');\n          \n          const pubDate = file.date ? `\\n      <pubDate>${new Date(file.date).toUTCString()}</pubDate>` : '';\n          \n          return `    <item>\n      <title>${escapeXml(file.title)}</title>\n      <link>${config.baseUrl}/${file.slug}.html</link>\n      <guid>${config.baseUrl}/${file.slug}.html</guid>\n      <description><![CDATA[${file.content}]]></description>${pubDate}\n      ${categories}\n    </item>`;\n        }).join('\\n');\n        \n        const feed = `<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<rss version=\"2.0\">\n  <channel>\n    <title>${escapeXml(config.title)}</title>\n    <link>${config.baseUrl}</link>\n    <description>${escapeXml(config.title)}</description>\n${items}\n  </channel>\n</rss>`;\n        \n        ctx.output.set('feed.xml', feed);\n      },\n    },\n  };\n}\n\nfunction escapeXml(s: string): string {\n  return s.replace(/&/g, '&amp;').replace(/</g, '&lt;').replace(/>/g, '&gt;').replace(/\"/g, '&quot;').replace(/'/g, '&apos;');\n}\n```\n\nWait, looking at the test:\n```typescript\nit('feed items include category tags from taxonomy', async () => {\n    const files = [\n      makeFile({ slug: 'post-1', title: 'Post 1', tags: ['javascript'], date: '2024-01-01' }),\n    ];\n    const tagMap = new Map<string, ContentFile[]>();\n    tagMap.set('javascript', [files[0]]);\n    const data = new Map<string, unknown>();\n    data.set('taxonomy-tags', tagMap);\n    const ctx = makeCtx(files, data);\n    await plugin.hooks.afterBuild!(ctx);\n    const feed = ctx.output.get('feed.xml')!;\n    expect(feed).toContain('<category>javascript</category>');\n});\n```\n\nSo the taxonomy data is already set in ctx.data by the test. My implementation reads from it correctly.\n\nFor tags lookup, since the tagMap stores ContentFile[] by tag name, I can efficiently find which tags a file has. But actually, I realize I should not look up by iterating over all tags - instead, let me look up directly from the file's own `tags` property.\n\nActually wait, the test creates a tag map with `javascript: [files[0]]`. The file has `tags: ['javascript']`. So I can just use the file's `tags` property directly:\n\n```typescript\nconst tags = file.tags || [];\nconst categories = tags.map(tag => `      <category>${escapeXml(tag)}</category>`).join('\\n');\n```\n\nThis is simpler and more reliable. Let me use that.\n\nFor the date sort test:\n```typescript\nit('feed items sorted by date newest first', async () => {\n    const files = [\n      makeFile({ slug: 'old', title: 'Old Post', date: '2023-01-01' }),\n      makeFile({ slug: 'new', title: 'New Post', date: '2024-06-15' }),\n      makeFile({ slug: 'mid', title: 'Mid Post', date: '2024-01-01' }),\n    ];\n    ...\n    const newIdx = feed.indexOf('New Post');\n    const midIdx = feed.indexOf('Mid Post');\n    const oldIdx = feed.indexOf('Old Post');\n    expect(newIdx).toBeLessThan(midIdx);\n    expect(midIdx).toBeLessThan(oldIdx);\n});\n```\n\nSo the order should be: \"New Post\", \"Mid Post\", \"Old Post\" - newest first.\n\nAnd for handling files without dates:\n```typescript\nit('handles files without dates', async () => {\n    const files = [\n      makeFile({ slug: 'no-date', title: 'No Date Post', content: '<p>No date</p>' }),\n    ];\n    ...\n    expect(feed).toContain('No Date Post');\n    expect(feed).not.toContain('<pubDate>undefined</pubDate>');\n});\n```\n\nSo files without dates should still appear, but not have `<pubDate>undefined</pubDate>`. My implementation handles this with the conditional.\n\nFor the link test:\n```typescript\nit('feed link uses baseUrl from config', async () => {\n    ...\n    expect(feed).toContain('<link>https://example.com</link>');\n    expect(feed).toContain('<link>https://example.com/post-1.html</link>');\n});\n```\n\nOK, my implementation should work.\n\n### search-index.ts\n\nTests:\n1. Generates `search-index.json` in ctx.output\n2. Index entries have `title`, `slug`, and `content` (plain text)\n3. HTML tags stripped from indexed content\n4. Code blocks included\n5. Empty file list → `[]`\n6. Valid JSON\n7. Entries include `tags` if present\n\nDependencies: `['markdown-ext', 'syntax-highlight']`\n\nImplementation:\n```typescript\nexport function createSearchIndexPlugin(): Plugin {\n  return {\n    name: 'search-index',\n    version: '1.0.0',\n    dependencies: ['markdown-ext', 'syntax-highlight'],\n    hooks: {\n      afterBuild(ctx: BuildContext): void {\n        const index = ctx.files.map(file => {\n          const entry: Record<string, unknown> = {\n            slug: file.slug,\n            title: file.title,\n            content: stripHtml(file.content),\n          };\n          if (file.tags && file.tags.length > 0) {\n            entry.tags = file.tags;\n          }\n          return entry;\n        });\n        \n        ctx.output.set('search-index.json', JSON.stringify(index));\n      },\n    },\n  };\n}\n\nfunction stripHtml(html: string): string {\n  return html.replace(/<[^>]*>/g, '');\n}\n```\n\nWait, looking at the test:\n```typescript\nit('HTML tags stripped from indexed content', async () => {\n    const files = [\n      makeFile({\n        slug: 'post',\n        title: 'Post',\n        content: '<h1>Title</h1><p>This is <strong>bold</strong> text.</p>',\n      }),\n    ];\n    ...\n    expect(index[0].content).not.toContain('<');\n    expect(index[0].content).not.toContain('>');\n    expect(index[0].content).toContain('bold');\n    expect(index[0].content).toContain('text');\n});\n```\n\nSo strip HTML tags. `stripHtml('<h1>Title</h1>')` → `'Title'`. `<p>This is <strong>bold</strong> text.</p>` → `'This is bold text.'`. Good.\n\nAnd code block content should be included:\n```typescript\nit('code blocks included in searchable text', async () => {\n    ...\n    content: '<p>Example:</p><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const x = 1;</code></pre>',\n    ...\n    expect(index[0].content).toContain('const x = 1;');\n});\n```\n\n`stripHtml` on that would give `'Example:const x = 1;'`. That contains `'const x = 1;'`. Good.\n\n### sitemap.ts\n\nTests:\n1. Generates `sitemap.xml` in ctx.output\n2. Includes all content page URLs (from ctx.output, excluding generated ones)\n3. Includes taxonomy tag page URLs\n4. Includes `feed.xml` URL\n5. URLs use baseUrl\n6. Valid XML structure\n7. Handles site with no tag pages\n\nDependencies: `['taxonomy', 'rss-feed']`\n\nThe sitemap should include URLs from ctx.output. But it shouldn't include `sitemap.xml` itself. Also, taxonomy tag pages are already in ctx.output (generated by taxonomy plugin). And `feed.xml` should be included if present.\n\nLooking at the test:\n```typescript\nit('includes all content page URLs', async () => {\n    const output = new Map<string, string>();\n    output.set('post-1.html', '<html></html>');\n    output.set('post-2.html', '<html></html>');\n    ...\n    expect(sitemap).toContain('https://example.com/post-1.html');\n    expect(sitemap).toContain('https://example.com/post-2.html');\n});\n\nit('includes taxonomy tag page URLs', async () => {\n    const output = new Map<string, string>();\n    output.set('tags/javascript.html', '<html>js tag page</html>');\n    const tagMap = new Map<string, ContentFile[]>();\n    tagMap.set('javascript', []);\n    const data = new Map<string, unknown>();\n    data.set('taxonomy-tags', tagMap);\n    ...\n    expect(sitemap).toContain('https://example.com/tags/javascript.html');\n});\n\nit('includes feed.xml URL', async () => {\n    const output = new Map<string, string>();\n    output.set('feed.xml', '<rss></rss>');\n    ...\n    expect(sitemap).toContain('https://example.com/feed.xml');\n});\n```\n\nSo basically, I iterate through ctx.output and add URLs for everything, including the sitemap.xml itself? Let me check... The test for content pages only adds `post-1.html` and `post-2.html` to output. The sitemap XML also adds a `sitemap.xml` key to output. But when checking for content page URLs, it checks for `post-1.html` and `post-2.html`.\n\nActually, I should include all URLs from ctx.output in the sitemap, but exlude the sitemap.xml itself (to avoid referencing itself, which is fine but might cause issues). Let me look at the test for \"handles site with no tag pages\":\n\n```typescript\nit('handles site with no tag pages', async () => {\n    const output = new Map<string, string>();\n    output.set('post.html', '<html></html>');\n    ...\n    expect(sitemap).toContain('https://example.com/post.html');\n    expect(sitemap).not.toContain('/tags/');\n});\n```\n\nOK so the sitemap just includes whatever is in ctx.output (excluding the sitemap itself). The test for tag pages uses a test that has tag pages in the output map AND taxonomy-tags data. But actually, the sitemap plugin should just look at what's in ctx.output and include it all (since the taxonomy plugin already added tag pages to ctx.output).\n\nWait, but I don't need to check taxonomy-tags data in the sitemap plugin - I just iterate through ctx.output and include all keys.\n\nActually wait, there's a subtlety. The test for tag pages says:\n```typescript\nexpect(sitemap).toContain('https://example.com/tags/javascript.html');\n```\n\nThis URL is from ctx.output which has `tags/javascript.html` as a key. So if I just iterate through ctx.output, the tag page URL would naturally be included. Good.\n\nBut what about `feed.xml`? It's also in ctx.output when the rss-feed plugin has run. So if I iterate through all ctx.output keys, feed.xml would be included too.\n\nBut I should exclude `sitemap.xml` from the URLs (don't include itself). Let me check if the test checks